1
00:00:22,025 --> 00:00:31,776
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is on the Vedāvitākasutta, which is the two types of thoughts that the Buddha talks about in Majjhima Nikaya.

2
00:00:32,757 --> 00:00:41,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the gist is that the Buddha realized that if he were to allow thoughts of greed, aversion, delusion to

3
00:00:43,695 --> 00:00:58,355
[SPEAKER_00]: To sort of make him act out of it, if he were to welcome them, if he were to entertain them, if he were to delight such arisen thoughts, it would lead to his affliction, to affliction of others, it would lead away from nibbāna.

4
00:00:59,551 --> 00:01:14,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, consequently, he realized that if a thought of renunciation arises, and if he were to allow it, if he were to welcome it, if he were to entertain it, if he were to cultivate it, it would not lead to his affliction and it would not lead away from nibbāna.

5
00:01:14,859 --> 00:01:18,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, he then started cultivating the thoughts of renunciation.

6
00:01:19,261 --> 00:01:20,721
[SPEAKER_00]: As you often mentioned in other talks,

7
00:01:23,153 --> 00:01:27,194
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about getting rid of the original thoughts, even if they're unwholesome.

8
00:01:27,874 --> 00:01:29,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about not welcoming them.

9
00:01:30,095 --> 00:01:36,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about not being careless in regard to their presence, while they're present.

10
00:01:37,797 --> 00:01:41,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this sutta, the Buddha says that...

11
00:01:43,173 --> 00:01:48,596
[SPEAKER_00]: As he would be abiding diligent, ardent and resolute, a thought of sensual desire would arise in him.

12
00:01:48,636 --> 00:01:53,158
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a thought would arise on its own, despite him being ardent and resolute.

13
00:01:54,078 --> 00:01:55,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not a problem.

14
00:01:55,339 --> 00:02:02,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not something he needs to get rid of, in a sense of that's not something he needs to prevent directly, or he can prevent directly from arising.

15
00:02:02,402 --> 00:02:03,083
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't do that.

16
00:02:03,943 --> 00:02:10,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but when the sensual... the thought of sensual desire arose, he would have... he understood thus.

17
00:02:11,205 --> 00:02:18,066
[SPEAKER_00]: This thought has arisen in me, leads to my own affliction, affliction of others, affliction of both, and so on, leads away from nibbāna.

18
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:22,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is something that needs to be contemplated.

19
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:26,868
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the affliction for me, affliction for others, affliction for both,

20
00:02:28,142 --> 00:02:34,608
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to be evident, certainly not going to be self-evident in the beginning, when somebody say they allow these thoughts to arise.

21
00:02:35,128 --> 00:02:38,891
[SPEAKER_00]: This nature is still kind of hidden, not seen, not understood.

22
00:02:38,931 --> 00:02:40,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where that diligence comes in.

23
00:02:41,373 --> 00:02:56,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while you're enduring the pressure of centrality, you don't act out of it, and then those thoughts that are around, you want to be able to have enough space to to start seeing their their affliction, their lack of merit, their

24
00:02:57,515 --> 00:02:59,956
[SPEAKER_00]: They're a misfortune, in that sense.

25
00:03:00,676 --> 00:03:04,038
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that the pressure of sensuality is unpleasant.

26
00:03:04,738 --> 00:03:14,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what that results in, is that then people immediately want to get rid of the thoughts because of their pain, and then they contemplate their affliction and their misery.

27
00:03:15,302 --> 00:03:18,764
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's all rooted in simply trying to deny the thoughts because they hurt you.

28
00:03:19,584 --> 00:03:23,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I keep saying the thoughts need to be endured first.

29
00:03:24,086 --> 00:03:32,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to have enough strength to have the pain of sensuality there, without clouding your reason, your judgment, and so on.

30
00:03:33,368 --> 00:03:48,892
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have developed samādhi to that extent, then you can start actually diligently reminding yourself, clarifying further, and developing that context of their affliction, how unbeneficial they are, and so on.

31
00:03:50,013 --> 00:03:57,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while the sensual... the thought of sensual desire was present in him, he would be considering

32
00:03:58,335 --> 00:04:05,702
[SPEAKER_00]: How the presence of this thought, that I'm under, that I'm enduring, that I'm not acting out of, is actually an affliction.

33
00:04:06,162 --> 00:04:09,425
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually detrimental to my well-being.

34
00:04:09,565 --> 00:04:12,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It actually leads away from my goal of nibbāna.

35
00:04:12,328 --> 00:04:18,873
[SPEAKER_00]: And while he would be considering that, the thought that has a reason on its own, of sensual desire, would subside.

36
00:04:19,634 --> 00:04:22,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you get rid of those thoughts.

37
00:04:23,218 --> 00:04:25,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, I must stop this thinking.

38
00:04:25,903 --> 00:04:26,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

39
00:04:26,905 --> 00:04:28,569
[SPEAKER_00]: One point to note from this sutta.

40
00:04:30,199 --> 00:04:43,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it goes into saying that whatever bhikkhu, whatever a person frequently thinks about, ponders upon, welcomes, delights, revolves around, that will become an inclination of his mind.

41
00:04:43,787 --> 00:04:55,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you allow your mind to frequently think thoughts of sensuality, or thoughts of ill-will, or thoughts of aversion, or thoughts of resistance, or thoughts of carelessness, that will become the inclination, the state of your mind.

42
00:04:56,129 --> 00:05:07,357
[SPEAKER_00]: In the most practical terms, the inclination of your mind really just means what your mind would be generally revolving around.

43
00:05:07,978 --> 00:05:14,843
[SPEAKER_00]: It means that those thoughts would be then coming more frequently because you have been welcoming them, delighting in them, and so on.

44
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:22,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the inclination of the mind determines your susceptibility to those types of thoughts.

45
00:05:23,266 --> 00:05:38,859
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, consequently, if you are diligent and make the effort to not welcome such type of thoughts for a, you know, sufficient amount of time, then those... the inclination of the mind will change, which means then those thoughts would be coming less and less frequently.

46
00:05:41,026 --> 00:05:58,299
[SPEAKER_00]: If you frequently allow your mind to think about thoughts of renunciation, contemplating benefits during your meditation, you contemplate benefits of giving things up, relinquishing, letting go of appropriations and ownership and similar, that will become the inclination of your mind.

47
00:05:58,859 --> 00:06:01,841
[SPEAKER_00]: If during your meditation you start considering how

48
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:08,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Solitude should be cultivated, should be developed, should be delighted in.

49
00:06:08,722 --> 00:06:18,985
[SPEAKER_00]: A company should be minimized, avoided, and you should basically be learning how to live with yourself and your own thoughts in solitude.

50
00:06:19,085 --> 00:06:20,866
[SPEAKER_00]: That would become the inclination of your mind.

51
00:06:21,806 --> 00:06:27,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's, you know, sometimes people might feel like, I have these thoughts keep coming up and I don't know how to deal with them.

52
00:06:27,528 --> 00:06:29,209
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not supposed to deal with the thoughts.

53
00:06:30,201 --> 00:06:44,373
[SPEAKER_00]: You're supposed to recognize them, the way they arise, and then see the mental state behind them, which is basically something you are responsible for, through prior welcoming, delighting, entertaining of such thoughts.

54
00:06:44,994 --> 00:06:55,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if those thoughts that arise are unwholesome, are pressuring, are unpleasant, you consider that their presence, their arising, is not in your control,

55
00:06:56,198 --> 00:07:04,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, if you welcome that arising, if you entertain that arising, you are leading yourself away from nibbāna and calming and peace.

56
00:07:05,281 --> 00:07:07,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thus, then, you dwell vigilant.

57
00:07:07,842 --> 00:07:08,702
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you can do.

58
00:07:08,742 --> 00:07:12,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, yeah, but what do I do then, so these thoughts disappear and they don't bother me.

59
00:07:13,424 --> 00:07:14,064
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you do.

60
00:07:14,244 --> 00:07:17,385
[SPEAKER_00]: You remain vigilant while those thoughts are here.

61
00:07:18,338 --> 00:07:32,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So that they don't lead you astray, and then they will subside on their own, and then that will be peaceful, and then you recognize, okay, so all I need to do, as we said in in those all those previous talks, and in that book as well, is not open the door, not let them in.

62
00:07:32,918 --> 00:07:34,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And the simile the Buddha gives, it's like

63
00:07:35,372 --> 00:07:45,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as in the last month of the rainy season, in autumn, when the crops thicken, a cowherd would guard his cows by constantly tapping and poking them on their side with a stick to check and curb them.

64
00:07:46,037 --> 00:07:46,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

65
00:07:46,697 --> 00:07:50,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he sees that he could be flogged, imprisoned, fined or blamed.

66
00:07:50,739 --> 00:07:56,861
[SPEAKER_00]: He could get in trouble if he were to let those cows stray into other people's crops.

67
00:07:57,541 --> 00:08:00,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to let your mind welcome, delight, entertain

68
00:08:01,503 --> 00:08:18,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And even more specifically, sight, sound, smell, taste, touches are other people's crops.

69
00:08:18,513 --> 00:08:19,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said.

70
00:08:19,494 --> 00:08:24,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because people would take what belongs to Mara, Mara would then do with them what he wants.

71
00:08:25,578 --> 00:08:27,359
[SPEAKER_00]: And what are the things that belong to Mara?

72
00:08:27,399 --> 00:08:28,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

73
00:08:30,352 --> 00:08:36,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person would be taking those up particularly because of the pleasures that one gets through those things.

74
00:08:37,419 --> 00:08:40,061
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, one is enslaved by mara's control.

75
00:08:40,781 --> 00:08:42,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't get rid of the crops.

76
00:08:42,443 --> 00:08:43,724
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't belong to you.

77
00:08:43,744 --> 00:08:47,307
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just there because, you know, they have reason on their own.

78
00:08:47,327 --> 00:08:48,188
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not your problem.

79
00:08:48,388 --> 00:08:54,894
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is you letting your mind going there carelessly and eating and enjoying things that don't belong to it.

80
00:08:55,294 --> 00:08:56,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what gets you in trouble.

81
00:08:57,035 --> 00:09:09,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this... in this sense, the... the shepherd, he... he realizes that all... he just needs to keep... keep check on his cows, because there is a danger for him if he lets them go astray.

82
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:10,420
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

83
00:09:11,001 --> 00:09:15,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to keep check on yourself, in regard to the thoughts that are presenting themselves to you.

84
00:09:16,583 --> 00:09:18,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And, see, the more you keep yourself in check,

85
00:09:19,390 --> 00:09:27,156
[SPEAKER_00]: The less you would be fueling these thoughts of desire and ill-will, which means, less and less, that would be an inclination of your mind.

86
00:09:27,776 --> 00:09:33,620
[SPEAKER_00]: It would not be as frequently an inclination of your mind as it is maybe now.

87
00:09:34,601 --> 00:09:39,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when those thoughts subside, and that's the point the Buddha then makes with a follow-up simile,

88
00:09:40,525 --> 00:10:00,982
[SPEAKER_00]: When there are no more crops, when, you know, it's out of season now, so you don't really need to guard cows how far they go, because there is no trouble, no trap, no issues that they can create because the crops are gone, then all you need to do is be mindful that the cows are there.

89
00:10:01,903 --> 00:10:08,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have sufficiently endured the pressuring thoughts of other people's crops,

90
00:10:09,548 --> 00:10:20,354
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pressuring thoughts of desire, ill will, sensuality, distraction, guarding your mind, not letting your mind welcome, delight, entertain and act out, i.e.

91
00:10:20,394 --> 00:10:21,735
[SPEAKER_00]: go into the other people's crops.

92
00:10:22,856 --> 00:10:31,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the thoughts of renunciation will become your norm, and thoughts of sensuality will be seen as dangerous, not welcoming, and it will be easier and easier to not welcome them.

93
00:10:32,341 --> 00:10:35,523
[SPEAKER_00]: When thoughts of renunciation become your norm, there is no danger.

94
00:10:37,220 --> 00:10:43,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the... you don't... you're not... you're not acting out of desire, you're not wanting anything that doesn't belong to you.

95
00:10:43,844 --> 00:10:49,127
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just thoughts arise on their own, and you realize, oh, these thoughts of... they don't want to go into...

96
00:10:49,187 --> 00:10:53,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say, imagine you have a cow that doesn't want to go in other people's crops, and now you know that.

97
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:58,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And now that cow listens to you, and obeys your will.

98
00:10:59,232 --> 00:11:03,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you don't need to constantly poke it, like how you used to have to do it, how you used to have to keep it reined in.

99
00:11:04,912 --> 00:11:07,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no more the same type of pull and pressure.

100
00:11:08,333 --> 00:11:10,934
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, all you need to do is know that the cow is there.

101
00:11:10,974 --> 00:11:16,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't just completely lose it out of your sight, because then it might, you know, decide to go somewhere where it shouldn't.

102
00:11:17,196 --> 00:11:30,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but you don't have to actively be restraining it and keeping an eye on it, in a sense of... it's enough for you to know that your mind is there, when it's in a... when it's having a thought of renunciation as it's known.

103
00:11:31,557 --> 00:11:40,760
[SPEAKER_00]: And there will be enough to protect that mind and yourself from any peril that could arise if you give in to thoughts of sensuality.

104
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:45,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, the simile then continues and says, the Buddha says, the...

105
00:11:45,921 --> 00:11:53,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when all the crops have been brought inside, cowhead would guard his cows while sterling at the root of a tree, but he only needs to be mindful that the cows are there.

106
00:11:53,863 --> 00:11:58,885
[SPEAKER_00]: He only needed to be mindful that his mind, the state of mind that is there, nothing else.

107
00:11:59,745 --> 00:12:07,056
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't need to worry about the thoughts, because he has replaced the thoughts of pressuring desires and ill-will.

108
00:12:08,138 --> 00:12:09,100
[SPEAKER_00]: They have subsided.

109
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:10,302
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how he replaced them.

110
00:12:10,642 --> 00:12:13,967
[SPEAKER_00]: They've subsided and then became replaced by the thoughts of renunciation.

111
00:12:15,516 --> 00:12:18,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Which was then seen as beneficial and non-dangerous.

112
00:12:18,139 --> 00:12:27,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when thoughts of renunciation are the thoughts that are arising for you, are the thoughts that you value, the state of mind behind that, you don't have to fear.

113
00:12:28,310 --> 00:12:29,992
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need to protect this closely.

114
00:12:30,452 --> 00:12:32,715
[SPEAKER_00]: You actually can relax within it.

115
00:12:32,895 --> 00:12:34,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's in itself a form of samādhi.

116
00:12:34,557 --> 00:12:35,057
[SPEAKER_00]: However,

117
00:12:36,175 --> 00:12:47,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Having still those thoughts come and go on their own, it's not as good of... as useful of a samādhi as it would be if you were to calm down further in regard to those thoughts.

118
00:12:48,943 --> 00:12:52,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where the sutta continues then.

119
00:12:52,185 --> 00:12:58,410
[SPEAKER_00]: If he were to think these thoughts of renunciation day and night, day and night, no affliction would arise for him or others.

120
00:12:58,650 --> 00:13:03,634
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no basis for any possibility of affliction to arise if those thoughts are his mode of being.

121
00:13:04,054 --> 00:13:12,500
[SPEAKER_00]: However, if he were to dwell thinking like that all the time, that would not be calming overall.

122
00:13:12,540 --> 00:13:14,801
[SPEAKER_00]: It would probably tire out his mind.

123
00:13:15,762 --> 00:13:20,983
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... tiredness means he's not quite, eh... composed as well as he could be.

124
00:13:23,023 --> 00:13:43,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So, before we go into, eh... how would you compose then, eh... it's actually important to note that this step of composing internally and calming down only applies after thoughts of sensuality, ill-will, eh... pressure, doubt, the five hindrances are not moving you anymore.

125
00:13:44,148 --> 00:14:04,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, only applies after you have been diligent, ardent, resolute, and only having thoughts of renunciation that revolve around in your mind, and when thoughts of other people's crops arise, they dissipate almost instantaneously, because you have not been welcoming and entertaining those thoughts prior to that long enough.

126
00:14:05,526 --> 00:14:06,707
[SPEAKER_00]: And, just as a reminder,

127
00:14:07,347 --> 00:14:14,868
[SPEAKER_00]: You have also not been trying to get rid of those thoughts because they were unpleasant, which would have been on the level of acting out of aversion.

128
00:14:16,129 --> 00:14:21,830
[SPEAKER_00]: If you haven't been doing it long enough, they will be subsiding and being... and become less and less frequent.

129
00:14:22,950 --> 00:14:34,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why I'm pointing this out is because sometimes a person can think, yes, I'm restrained, I'm thinking thoughts of sensuality, I'm thinking thoughts of renunciation, that's good, but now I also want to not think anything.

130
00:14:35,327 --> 00:14:53,113
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem with you sort of trying to do it, as opposed to being a natural next step that will occur after thoughts of renunciation has been established as your mode of being, is that your attempts would be basically rooted in still trying to get rid of the arising of thoughts.

131
00:14:53,694 --> 00:15:00,076
[SPEAKER_00]: You would try to get rid of that aspect of thought coming on its own and bothering you.

132
00:15:00,316 --> 00:15:02,597
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you haven't quite dealt

133
00:15:03,177 --> 00:15:08,480
[SPEAKER_00]: With the bothering aspect of the thoughts, which is why you want to get rid of them through jhāna and so on.

134
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:14,122
[SPEAKER_00]: But jhāna will come when you stop trying to get rid of thoughts, but learn how not to be moved by them.

135
00:15:14,562 --> 00:15:21,386
[SPEAKER_00]: In order not to be moved by them, you need to have thoroughly, sufficiently developed thoughts of renunciation,

136
00:15:22,046 --> 00:15:32,779
[SPEAKER_00]: The benefits of renunciation, the value, the peace, the non-peril, the non-danger, all of those values need to be something you frequently reflect upon.

137
00:15:33,379 --> 00:15:36,663
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yes, even if you get tired on account of it, it's good tiredness.

138
00:15:36,723 --> 00:15:38,185
[SPEAKER_00]: It's tiredness from a good work.

139
00:15:39,060 --> 00:15:43,722
[SPEAKER_00]: The time will come when you will recognize that now you don't even have to do that good work anymore.

140
00:15:44,123 --> 00:15:47,284
[SPEAKER_00]: But until that time comes on its own, you just have to.

141
00:15:47,304 --> 00:15:49,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's tiring and sometimes unpleasant.

142
00:15:50,246 --> 00:16:01,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you try to do it sooner, it's gonna be on the level of still that view, that it would be better if I don't have these thoughts, because I still, on some level, don't want to deal with the bothering aspects.

143
00:16:02,011 --> 00:16:03,732
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you are actually bothered.

144
00:16:04,153 --> 00:16:06,794
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, your attempts at, eh...

145
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:19,570
[SPEAKER_00]: It's stealing your mind internally, and sort of surmounting the domain of thinking and pondering in the first jhāna, that still has thinking and pondering, but now you're completely unbothered.

146
00:16:19,950 --> 00:16:24,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that will remain unreachable, because you still act out of craving against thoughts.

147
00:16:25,515 --> 00:16:32,739
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to still your mind internally into that first jhāna and the second jhāna, you need to have no craving present.

148
00:16:33,439 --> 00:16:54,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to have sufficiently, thoroughly, not acted out of craving, dwelt on the perceptions of danger in regard to sensual thoughts, thoughts of ill-will, thoughts of distraction, until that, you know, the danger of other people's crops, until the mind calms down on its own, not until you feel like you want to enter jhāna now.

149
00:16:55,645 --> 00:17:03,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why every jhāna description starts by having been withdrawn from sensuality, he experienced pleasure on account of it.

150
00:17:04,389 --> 00:17:09,871
[SPEAKER_00]: The citta viveka of the mind is the viveka, it's the seclusion from unwholesome states.

151
00:17:10,644 --> 00:17:18,270
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind recognizes that it's thoroughly secluded, the joy, pleasure is born on account of it, and then he enters and abides in jhāna.

152
00:17:18,931 --> 00:17:20,212
[SPEAKER_00]: And we pointed it before.

153
00:17:20,272 --> 00:17:24,055
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like I do something, I enter jhāna, pleasure of jhāna comes to me.

154
00:17:24,195 --> 00:17:24,555
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

155
00:17:24,935 --> 00:17:33,002
[SPEAKER_00]: I do something, meaning I see the danger in the entire domain of central thoughts, thoughts of ill-will, thoughts of distraction, the entire domain of the five hindrances.

156
00:17:33,782 --> 00:17:37,065
[SPEAKER_00]: I contemplate, I work on sustaining the perception of that danger.

157
00:17:37,565 --> 00:17:39,327
[SPEAKER_00]: I am not negligent in regard to it.

158
00:17:39,928 --> 00:17:42,590
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how I seclude myself from it.

159
00:17:42,650 --> 00:17:46,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure of safety from those dangerous states.

160
00:17:46,533 --> 00:17:48,955
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you will feel safe now if you

161
00:17:50,616 --> 00:17:59,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, if the crops have kind of been taken away, there is no more risk, there are no more temptations for the cow, you feel relieved.

162
00:17:59,399 --> 00:18:01,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the pleasure of the first jhāna.

163
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:01,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Relief.

164
00:18:02,360 --> 00:18:03,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Relief from that burden.

165
00:18:04,941 --> 00:18:10,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you do the work in terms of seeing the danger, protect the mind from leaping into those thoughts of danger,

166
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:15,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when that calms down, you will experience the relief.

167
00:18:15,761 --> 00:18:22,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pleasure that's usually assorted with the pleasure of entering the jhāna, it's actually the pleasure of withdrawal from unwholesome states.

168
00:18:24,102 --> 00:18:27,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unwholesome states gives you the pleasure of relief.

169
00:18:28,543 --> 00:18:39,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And when that pleasure of relief is something you become used to, that's how you enter the jhāna and surmount thinking and pondering, and that's how you can instill your mind internally.

170
00:18:39,905 --> 00:18:41,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no shortcut.

171
00:18:42,008 --> 00:18:43,649
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just enter the jhāna.

172
00:18:43,669 --> 00:19:00,543
[SPEAKER_00]: They will then bypass the necessity for thoroughly seeing and perceiving and sustaining the perception of danger and affliction in thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of will and thoughts of distraction.

173
00:19:01,764 --> 00:19:02,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the work.

174
00:19:02,965 --> 00:19:08,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you will most likely, practically speaking, need to get tired first.

175
00:19:09,413 --> 00:19:26,257
[SPEAKER_00]: From constant, constantly sustaining and protecting your mind within, like, thoughts of renunciation, thoughts of giving up, thoughts of non-desire, thoughts of nibbāna, up until, like, sensuality, ill-will and, and, uh, distraction doesn't pressure you as much.

176
00:19:27,605 --> 00:19:42,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, with that kind of new space you created, through that sort of relief, you can... okay, now let me... let me... let me relieve myself further from... from the... from the duty of having to kind of sustain the thoughts of renunciation.

177
00:19:43,236 --> 00:19:49,740
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will be able to then do so, because, again, the confidence will come, because now you see that these thoughts of sensuality will

178
00:19:50,661 --> 00:19:51,542
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not as frequent.

179
00:19:52,422 --> 00:19:53,223
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not as pressuring.

180
00:19:53,403 --> 00:19:54,243
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm more distant.

181
00:19:54,303 --> 00:19:56,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the crops are far away.

182
00:19:56,665 --> 00:20:02,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't need to be, like, every second keeping an eye on whether your cow is welcoming the idea of it and trying to go there.

183
00:20:03,629 --> 00:20:05,110
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can give them some space.

184
00:20:05,330 --> 00:20:11,134
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you give them some space, you learn then just how to see the state of mind without needing to police every single movement of the cow.

185
00:20:12,094 --> 00:20:17,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, within that, okay, now let me just calm down and sort of even drop those cows, so to speak.

186
00:20:18,047 --> 00:20:43,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Or actually, even more accurately, so... calm those cows, eh... sit them down, stop them from moving, because if the cows that you need to keep an eye on, are laying down, still, motionless, not moving, there is not even a possibility of them going to the crops, for as long as they don't get up and start walking around.

187
00:20:43,745 --> 00:20:46,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not even having the immediate possibility,

188
00:20:56,550 --> 00:21:07,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the danger, the temptation, has been removed sufficiently enough, so that viveka, of the first jhāna, seclusion from unwholesome state, has been developed sufficiently enough.

189
00:21:08,077 --> 00:21:11,059
[SPEAKER_00]: What is sufficient, you will know only once you develop it.

190
00:21:11,119 --> 00:21:13,521
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't decide beforehand, oh, this feels sufficient.

191
00:21:14,381 --> 00:21:20,502
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sufficient when those thoughts are too distant, when those thoughts have lost their power, thoughts of sensuality, of will and distraction.

192
00:21:21,162 --> 00:21:34,045
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sufficient when, basically, thoughts of nibbāna, thoughts of renunciation, thoughts of giving up, thoughts of solitude, provide you with pleasure, delight, and that fatigue that comes on account of them is also something you enjoy.

193
00:21:34,245 --> 00:21:35,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

194
00:21:35,625 --> 00:21:38,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the Buddha said that fatigue was not conducive for

195
00:21:41,134 --> 00:21:44,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Developing samādhi, developing composure, that's only relatively speaking.

196
00:21:45,177 --> 00:22:00,545
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... that fatigue is not conducive for developing that samādhi and composure from the point of view of jhāna, but it's very conducive for developing samādhi and composure from the point of view of, you know, previous fatigues of centrality of will and acting out and being caught up in that whole mess.

197
00:22:02,270 --> 00:22:15,893
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in other suttas it is said that a skillful, diligent monk should replace the joys of a household life with the joys of renunciation, and that he should also replace the pains of a household life with the pains of renunciation.

198
00:22:16,453 --> 00:22:19,254
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep saying it needs to become your mode of being.

199
00:22:19,494 --> 00:22:30,497
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to become... your thoughts need to be, by default, preferring and valuing renunciation and giving up, as opposed to sensuality and engagement and a household life.

200
00:22:31,627 --> 00:22:43,831
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would reach the state of that preference, where you would prefer the thoughts of renunciation, as your default mental states, through diligence.

201
00:22:44,151 --> 00:23:00,017
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through diligently applying yourself, not skipping a beat, in terms of contemplating the danger of sensuality, the burden of sensuality, all the drawbacks of acting out and giving in to the pressure, certainly not going to be developed

202
00:23:00,517 --> 00:23:04,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Through the, you know, like doing meditation techniques and watching your nostrils, quite the opposite.

203
00:23:04,620 --> 00:23:11,466
[SPEAKER_00]: You read the suttas and we would say, day and night, monk... a monk purifies his mind from the unwholesome states.

204
00:23:12,346 --> 00:23:19,292
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that purification of the mind, it's exactly on the level of that contemplation of the danger, seeing how those states have arisen, how they cease.

205
00:23:19,913 --> 00:23:26,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, how they persist and how they pressure you and not acting out of that pressure, enduring it, developing a recognition

206
00:23:27,331 --> 00:23:42,654
[SPEAKER_00]: That if you haven't been acting out long enough out of those states, you will start developing a recognition of safety, because you realize, yeah, it's unpleasant, it's pressuring, but actually, this, in the in the center of the experience of being pressured by those thoughts, that's where the safety is.

207
00:23:43,095 --> 00:23:49,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, safety is you not reaching out towards those those thoughts and objects that they present, not acting out.

208
00:23:49,936 --> 00:23:53,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ironically, the safety is within that pressure.

209
00:23:53,838 --> 00:23:57,880
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, habitually acting out of pressure prevented you from ever seeing this.

210
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:08,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the simile of the of the turtle, where a jackal comes and tries to bite its limbs, jackal being Mara, turtle being the Buddha, and the turtle just retracts all its limbs into the shell.

211
00:24:08,785 --> 00:24:09,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And jackal can't do anything.

212
00:24:09,825 --> 00:24:14,928
[SPEAKER_00]: He might wait and wait and wait, but eventually he'll have to give it up, because no limbs are coming out.

213
00:24:15,088 --> 00:24:17,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not reaching out, it stayed within.

214
00:24:17,869 --> 00:24:24,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, that's how the mind is stilled.

215
00:24:24,571 --> 00:24:44,318
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is stilled when the danger in those unwholesome states is sufficiently dwelt on, for as long as it takes, not given in, not welcomed, not delighted, not acted out of, when thoughts of renunciation become your norm, and when your diligence, despite that fatigue of thinking, remains unchanged.

216
00:24:45,547 --> 00:24:53,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you have done your work on that level, then you will be able to afford calming your mind within that.

217
00:24:53,772 --> 00:25:03,118
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Guru said in previous sutta, that it's impossible for a monk to attain the jhāna unless he has fully understood the gratification, the danger and the escape in regard to central thoughts.

218
00:25:04,338 --> 00:25:06,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this applies the same here.

